14
00:01:05,350 --> 00:01:10,789
G, today you and I will be talking about AI supply chain security.

15
00:01:10,789 --> 00:01:16,630
Software supply chain security is a topic that we have covered many times on the podcast,

16
00:01:16,630 --> 00:01:23,830
most recently episode 712 on SIG store, but we have not covered AI supply chain security.

17
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:29,910
Let's start out with some basic definitions.

18
00:01:29,910 --> 00:01:32,789
We're talking about AI in the supply chain. What are we talking about?

19
00:01:32,799 --> 00:01:45,590
Okay, so I think it's important to quickly note what we mean by the software supply chain to start with, which is the supply chain analogies referring to when we're building things, construction and stuff.

20
00:01:45,590 --> 00:01:49,270
And it's what each part of that contributes to the next part.

21
00:01:49,270 --> 00:01:55,990
And in software, we have a similar model, although in reality, it's more of a directional graph than it is a chain.

22
00:01:55,990 --> 00:01:57,670
But that's a whole another topic that I'm sure you've covered already.

23
00:01:57,680 --> 00:02:07,990
The key difference, I think, with the AI software supply chain is that we're no longer producing source code to binary deterministic artifacts.

24
00:02:07,990 --> 00:02:17,190
We're not shipping something that's a translation of instructions that when you run it, it does basically it should do what you've written down.

25
00:02:17,190 --> 00:02:20,309
I know there's nuance and things that can creep in.

26
00:02:20,309 --> 00:02:29,271
But in AI, it becomes entirely non-deterministic like the relationship between the input and the output. It's not binary at all.

27
00:02:29,271 --> 00:02:32,470
It's a probabilistic model.

28
00:02:32,470 --> 00:02:35,110
It turns intent into content.

29
00:02:35,110 --> 00:02:36,630
And that content can be lots of different things.

30
00:02:36,630 --> 00:02:48,470
In generative AI, the most popular things that we see are LLMs, which is what unless you've been living under a rock for the last 2 years, you've probably heard about it just about everywhere.

31
00:02:48,470 --> 00:02:57,990
Those are large language models which they're designed to predict the next word or the next token actually, but we'll just say word for simplicity in a string of text.

32
00:02:57,990 --> 00:03:06,790
So you give it one part of text and it tells you what the next word is most likely to be and you keep going until you have entire large amounts of text.

33
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:13,190
And we've got really good at turning that into things that are actually useful.

34
00:03:13,190 --> 00:03:23,110
There's other types of AI obviously as well machine learning other types of generative AI but this is what most people are talking about when they talk about AI now are talking about large language models.

35
00:03:23,110 --> 00:03:34,390
Somewhere a few years ago, I think between the GP2 to GPT3 time frame, we sort of became aware, not entirely on purpose, but it wasn't really accidental either.

36
00:03:34,390 --> 00:03:41,350
And it's hard to know exactly because some of these frontier labs are not entirely forthcoming with their research.

37
00:03:41,350 --> 00:03:51,750
But we realized that the content that we can create with large language models could actually quite well include software code.

38
00:03:51,750 --> 00:03:56,390
And from software code, we sort of went on to chain of reasoning and then tool use and now we have agents.

39
00:03:56,390 --> 00:04:01,030
So it's not just now content that is a story or written pros or anything.

40
00:04:01,030 --> 00:04:05,110
It's content that actually ends up itself being executable.

41
00:04:05,110 --> 00:04:17,990
So we've gone all the way from intent now to actual execution of code and we can chain these systems together to basically at the point now do almost anything that humans can do on a computer.

42
00:04:17,990 --> 00:04:21,670
So we've got full computer use now.

43
00:04:21,670 --> 00:04:29,189
varying levels of success and massive amounts of supply chain complexity creeps in when you start thinking about this because in traditional security vulnerabilities,

44
00:04:29,199 --> 00:04:38,150
the sort of basis of them all in many cases at least is the ability to influence the control plane from the data plane.

45
00:04:38,150 --> 00:04:40,390
When that separation breaks down,

46
00:04:40,390 --> 00:04:51,110
that gives people who have access to the data plane access to the control plane and then they can change what a piece of software does to make it actually do things that are different from the intent.

47
00:04:51,110 --> 00:04:56,710
In this new model, we effectively merge the control plane and the data plane entirely,

48
00:04:56,710 --> 00:05:07,110
which means protecting the control plane from the data plane becomes much more complex both in the supply chain and during execution.

49
00:05:07,110 --> 00:05:14,469
I may know what you mean, but could you explain what you do mean when you say merging the control plane with the data plane in this new model?

50
00:05:14,479 --> 00:05:17,590
Yeah, absolutely.

51
00:05:17,590 --> 00:05:21,830
So the control plane in software, it's what tells the software what to do.

52
00:05:21,830 --> 00:05:28,870
So it's the instructions that the CPU executes or it's the interpreted language that the interpreter executes.

53
00:05:28,870 --> 00:05:33,590
It's the actual instructions.

54
00:05:33,590 --> 00:05:36,310
In AI, the control plane is the data plane.

55
00:05:36,310 --> 00:05:41,110
It's because the input to the model effectively controls what the output is going to be.

56
00:05:41,110 --> 00:05:50,070
So when that output is used in ways to actually control other things, when it's used to generate code,

57
00:05:50,070 --> 00:05:59,029
that input is both the data and the control because it's saying what's the next token should be, what should this look like, you're giving it intent rather than instructions.

58
00:05:59,029 --> 00:06:10,710
And when that's the weights inside the model that determine what that output is going to look like have as much influence on that or if not more even really than the prompt that you're giving it.

59
00:06:10,710 --> 00:06:16,950
So the data you're feeding into it and the data that it's been trained on affect what comes out of there.

60
00:06:16,950 --> 00:06:27,270
And then when we start introducing things like tool calling and retrieval augmented generation where data is actually added from external sources at runtime.

61
00:06:27,270 --> 00:06:38,469
These external sources then also effectively go into that merged control and data plane where the content in them if not handled extremely carefully can influence what that output looks like.

62
00:06:38,479 --> 00:06:43,830
Let's talk a little bit about the topology of the supply chain.

63
00:06:43,830 --> 00:06:49,749
One type of supply chain is you have code in GitHub.

64
00:06:49,749 --> 00:06:56,230
You push it out maybe compile package it up and deploy it onto some servers on the cloud or it's a hosted application.

65
00:06:56,230 --> 00:07:01,670
there's something like that for AI, but we're also running AIs on everybody's phone, their computers.

66
00:07:01,670 --> 00:07:07,189
What are the different types of supply chains and what are the stops along the way?

67
00:07:07,199 --> 00:07:17,830
So, almost all of them start with pre-training, which is there's got to be a model created at some point for any of this.

68
00:07:17,830 --> 00:07:20,790
And so, for a model to be created, there has to be data.

69
00:07:20,790 --> 00:07:26,230
So pre-training is when we're collecting data that can be used to actually train the model.

70
00:07:26,230 --> 00:07:32,390
So it's run through the training process and it's used to set the weights through that training process.

71
00:07:32,390 --> 00:07:36,469
It's incredibly complicated maths there that I cannot explain.

72
00:07:36,469 --> 00:07:42,150
And so at that stage there's really where does that data come from is the big question.

73
00:07:42,150 --> 00:07:46,230
So there's data that could be your own data that you collect yourself.

74
00:07:46,230 --> 00:07:48,950
It could be data from inside your company.

75
00:07:48,950 --> 00:07:56,629
And when you do that, you've really got to worry about like first of all formatting that data so it's usable, but also what's in that data.

76
00:07:56,629 --> 00:08:07,830
Like if you're using confidential data from inside your company, there's a high risk that that data could leak through a model because we don't really have complete deterministic control over what the model does.

77
00:08:07,830 --> 00:08:12,790
And so that needs to be sanitized or run through some privacy preserving process.

78
00:08:12,790 --> 00:08:24,869
The more common case though, and this is where the real supply chain in models that most of us are using day-to-day begins, is with these predetermined data sets made available by third parties.

79
00:08:24,869 --> 00:08:26,469
There's some proprietary ones.

80
00:08:26,469 --> 00:08:28,230
There's some very large public ones.

81
00:08:28,230 --> 00:08:37,589
There's things like common crawl which is basically a collection of almost all the data from the internet which is often the basis of other data sets where

82
00:08:37,589 --> 00:08:45,509
common crawl is taken and itself is sanitized and put into smaller data sets that are actually more useful for training.

83
00:08:45,509 --> 00:08:49,590
Hugging face has a huge repository of data sets on there that can be used for training.

84
00:08:49,600 --> 00:08:59,350
They also have a library, I think it's a Python library, might be available in other languages called data sets which allows you to bring data sets in from hugging face.

85
00:08:59,350 --> 00:09:02,550
I think it lets you do that entirely automatically just by name.

86
00:09:02,550 --> 00:09:04,470
I don't believe there's any verification in there.

87
00:09:04,470 --> 00:09:08,630
So that's a interesting step there that you need to handle outside of that.

88
00:09:08,630 --> 00:09:15,110
So the next step after that is the training itself and that is when the model is actually created.

89
00:09:15,110 --> 00:09:23,430
Now after that there's fine-tuning and alignment which is taking the trained model and trying to get it to behave in a certain way.

90
00:09:23,430 --> 00:09:29,110
So with alignment we're really talking about alignment to like human values or like don't be awful.

91
00:09:29,110 --> 00:09:32,230
There might be some pretty terrible things in some of these training sets.

92
00:09:32,230 --> 00:09:38,150
You can make every effort to remove them beforehand, but the chance of bias creeping in unintentionally is extremely high.

93
00:09:38,150 --> 00:09:49,670
And so during that alignment process, you can make efforts to fine-tune the model to show it what good structured output looks like and it not including certain topics and things like that.

94
00:09:49,670 --> 00:09:54,389
A lot of people will start with a base model and then we'll fine-tune it themselves.

95
00:09:54,389 --> 00:10:02,630
So there's models that are distributed that are only very lightly fine-tuned and the expectation is that you then fine-tune that model yourself.

96
00:10:02,630 --> 00:10:13,190
There's obviously models that are distributed that are entirely ready to use in chat bots or there's a bunch of training sets out there that let you do chain of reasoning and

97
00:10:13,190 --> 00:10:16,790
things like that where the model comes already trained with that and so you can use it.

98
00:10:16,790 --> 00:10:25,750
There's libraries and there's structured languages that match those training sets and so the models already for lack of better word talks in that format that you want.

99
00:10:25,750 --> 00:10:30,150
The next sort of stage after that I guess is the deployment of the model.

100
00:10:30,150 --> 00:10:31,829
So that's then taking those models.

101
00:10:31,829 --> 00:10:34,630
There's all kinds of public model registries out there.

102
00:10:34,640 --> 00:10:38,230
Hugging face again is probably the biggest one.

103
00:10:38,230 --> 00:10:40,069
There's commercial entities running their own model registries.

104
00:10:40,079 --> 00:10:43,590
There's companies running them inside the company.

105
00:10:43,590 --> 00:10:51,750
I believe Red Hat actually has a repository on hugging face of validated models and then it's deploying those models onto your infrastructure.

106
00:10:51,760 --> 00:10:56,550
Some sort of inference server is usually the standard way.

107
00:10:56,550 --> 00:11:02,069
They could also be like you mentioned deployed on edge devices depending on the size of the model and so on.

108
00:11:02,069 --> 00:11:04,069
I missed a entire step there.

109
00:11:04,069 --> 00:11:13,269
There's quantization and compression and preparing the model as well actually for deployment which so quantization is probably the most common thing that we see happening there.

110
00:11:13,269 --> 00:11:25,269
It's basically some of these models are huge and so one way to get them small in fact they're all huge the L in large language model which literally standing the first L large.

111
00:11:25,269 --> 00:11:27,590
But one of the ways to get them smaller is to quantize them,

112
00:11:27,590 --> 00:11:38,069
which if you're familiar with quantization in any other domain like in musical theory or something where notes are snapped to a grid, it's a similar concept with the models.

113
00:11:38,069 --> 00:11:46,230
So the weights are usually represented as like 32-bit floatingoint integers and so that precision can be reduced.

114
00:11:46,230 --> 00:11:52,949
So there's mathematical methods to take that 32-bit number and make it say a 16- bit number or even a four or 8 bit number.

115
00:11:52,949 --> 00:11:57,430
And when you do that, obviously you do lose some performance of the model.

116
00:11:57,430 --> 00:12:04,710
But we're getting better and better at actually measuring that performance and keeping that performance loss down to a minimum.

117
00:12:04,710 --> 00:12:08,790
But so that's a really effective way to get the model size smaller.

118
00:12:08,790 --> 00:12:15,190
And the smaller the model, then the less memory effectively that you need to run that model for inference.

119
00:12:15,190 --> 00:12:19,750
So if you've got a lot of processing power, a lot of RAM on GPUs, you can run much larger models.

120
00:12:19,750 --> 00:12:25,269
If you don't, if you're using it on a laptop or even a mobile phone, then you want a much simpler, smaller model.

121
00:12:25,269 --> 00:12:29,269
And then there's actually deploying the models as well.

122
00:12:29,269 --> 00:12:32,550
So that might be pre-eploying it in the case of like a handset device or something like that.

123
00:12:32,550 --> 00:12:42,950
That might be spinning models up at runtime in Kubernetes clusters and that kind of thing that goes through your traditional admission control and that kind of thing. Then it's running.

124
00:12:42,950 --> 00:12:52,710
We are familiar with the reasons that attackers go after the supply chain in existing or conventional software.

125
00:12:52,710 --> 00:12:59,030
Maybe trying to gain access to a system, egress data, steal something.

126
00:12:59,030 --> 00:13:02,230
In the case of AI supply chain attacks, what's in it for the attacker?

127
00:13:02,240 --> 00:13:09,030
So, that's a great question and there's a few different motivations.

128
00:13:09,030 --> 00:13:19,990
So the most common motivation, the one that we've actually seen in the wild already multiple times, is actually very similar to your traditional software supply chain attack.

129
00:13:19,990 --> 00:13:22,230
It's just trying to gain access to people's systems.

130
00:13:22,230 --> 00:13:25,750
Some of the training infrastructure is incredibly large and powerful.

131
00:13:25,750 --> 00:13:29,430
So getting access to people's training infrastructure gives you access to all of their GPUs.

132
00:13:29,430 --> 00:13:33,350
It can also get access to their private data and that kind of thing.

133
00:13:33,350 --> 00:13:42,230
The software itself used for model training has rapidly become a more important source of vulnerabilities over the last few years as attackers have realized this.

134
00:13:42,240 --> 00:13:48,710
So the first angle of attack, well I'll call it the first is obviously there's no real order.

135
00:13:48,710 --> 00:13:50,310
It's up to the attackers what they want to do.

136
00:13:50,310 --> 00:13:57,110
But that traditional attack looks to exploit vulnerabilities in that training software stack itself.

137
00:13:57,110 --> 00:14:05,430
And so if they're able to compromise those data training sets or if you're getting data training sets from the internet with no verification or

138
00:14:05,430 --> 00:14:09,590
understanding of where they've come from or anything like that, they can be entirely malicious.

139
00:14:09,590 --> 00:14:18,790
Some of these data sets are actually executable code like they're actually come as like Python pickle objects or other formats that

140
00:14:18,790 --> 00:14:27,990
require serialization that while you expect it to be loading inmemory objects for your training data, you can just embed Python methods or anything like that in there and they'll just execute.

141
00:14:27,990 --> 00:14:30,629
So that's very easy for attackers to put back doors in that.

142
00:14:30,639 --> 00:14:32,629
Sometimes that's expected.

143
00:14:32,629 --> 00:14:39,750
It's highly documented that that can be the case and that you should never load untrusted data into some of these pieces of software.

144
00:14:39,750 --> 00:14:48,629
In other cases, they've actually made efforts to mitigate that and then people have found bugs in that mitigation and those have been exploited as well.

145
00:14:48,629 --> 00:14:57,910
There was a really interesting paper, I think it was only late last year, which is about 200 years in AI time, but just a few months in human time,

146
00:14:57,910 --> 00:15:02,949
from the CSRO Australia, a bunch of researchers there actually started cataloging some of the security vulnerabilities that

147
00:15:02,949 --> 00:15:11,910
the internet had seen in some of the more popular machine learning frameworks and like how the rise of them had just exponentially gone up,

148
00:15:11,910 --> 00:15:16,710
the amount of reported vulnerabilities and the types of vulnerabilities that people were seeing there,

149
00:15:16,710 --> 00:15:28,069
which was I mean again, it's what You would expect as software gets more popular and it hasn't traditionally been written to consider some of these adversarial attacks against it, you'll rapidly will see those attacks go up.

150
00:15:28,079 --> 00:15:35,829
And we've also seen that in the wild multiple times there's been compromised data sets uploaded to Hugging Face where they've just basically been malware.

151
00:15:35,839 --> 00:15:39,189
They've embedded Python code that was just straight up malware.

152
00:15:39,189 --> 00:15:46,389
There was a really large public campaign, I think it was called Shadow Ray and then Shadow Ray 2.0.

153
00:15:46,389 --> 00:15:53,910
The original Shadow Ray campaign, I think, was quite small, but basically they're looking to exploit these kind of vulnerabilities to install crypto miners,

154
00:15:53,910 --> 00:15:58,710
which you can imagine some of this training infrastructure is pretty attractive for running crypto miners on.

155
00:15:58,710 --> 00:16:00,870
You can get yourself some Bitcoin from that.

156
00:16:00,870 --> 00:16:06,389
And then the Shadow Ray 2.0 was essentially the same thing, but by then it was entirely commoditized.

157
00:16:06,389 --> 00:16:17,910
So now we've actually seen this in like the public exploit and bot kits and stuff out there so that anyone can pick these up and off the shelf go and make your own version of it to try and steal people's GPU cycles.

158
00:16:17,910 --> 00:16:28,550
So the more interesting one though and the much harder to detect and understand because we're still in the era of emergent properties coming from AI models.

159
00:16:28,560 --> 00:16:30,470
They're surprising us.

160
00:16:30,470 --> 00:16:41,030
There's active research into understanding them and explainability and there's new things being discovered about them that are not properties that we designed them to have but it turns out they're there.

161
00:16:41,030 --> 00:16:51,350
And so with that in mind, the more interesting attack is influencing the way that the model will actually work based on the data.

162
00:16:51,350 --> 00:17:00,790
So, it's largely known as data poisoning, but there's actually a lot more nuance to it than just simply putting in like bad data, which absolutely does happen.

163
00:17:00,790 --> 00:17:03,110
Like we've seen things like I forget the name of it,

164
00:17:03,110 --> 00:17:15,510
but there's some researchers and they figured out how to like just make tiny changes to images for instance that when AI models either process them or if you trained on them, it did something completely different to what you'd think the image would actually do.

165
00:17:15,510 --> 00:17:24,949
So like if it was labeled by a human to start with, if it's like labeled as a cat, but the model if it's trained on it now, it'll start like saying all laptops are cats and that kind of thing.

166
00:17:24,949 --> 00:17:31,830
I think there was some people that actually used that attack in the wild against self-driving cars. I say in the wild, it wasn't actually used maliciously.

167
00:17:31,830 --> 00:17:40,789
It was just done to prove a point, but I think they were able to make self-driving cars like swerve off a road by putting a specific bumper sticker on another car or something like that.

168
00:17:40,789 --> 00:17:52,870
But I think that really paints the potential danger of these kind of attacks, especially with them being so difficult to detect when these AI systems are used in such safety critical ways.

169
00:17:52,870 --> 00:17:55,510
And we really don't know exactly how they're going to behave.

170
00:17:55,510 --> 00:18:02,710
And the other one that's really interesting there that's also now old in AI years but

171
00:18:02,710 --> 00:18:10,549
new in human years from I think it was also just late last year was attacking the quantization phase where people were able to have a base model.

172
00:18:10,559 --> 00:18:16,950
So one of the ways of sort of detecting what the models do is known as evaluations.

173
00:18:16,950 --> 00:18:25,669
So the safety and security evaluations which is essentially running the model a lot of times with predetermined taxonomy of like inputs and

174
00:18:25,669 --> 00:18:35,110
observing what the outputs are ranking them looking is it saying did I ask it to write code and it actually gave me like this essay on I don't know something horrible instead or it's not that

175
00:18:35,110 --> 00:18:43,510
simple there's obviously more to it than that but that kind of thing they were able to produce a model that it passed all those safety evaluations and like it appeared in all intents and

176
00:18:43,520 --> 00:18:49,510
purposes to be quite functional and safe and everything.

177
00:18:49,510 --> 00:18:55,830
However, when quantized, it actually turns out that then the malicious payload that was in the training data could be triggered.

178
00:18:55,830 --> 00:19:00,230
And so they could distribute this model and it was appeared to be fine.

179
00:19:00,230 --> 00:19:03,990
You could put all the safety evaluations on there, sign them and everything looks great.

180
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:11,430
Someone else takes it, quantizes it to put it on their smaller device and runs it and now it's vulnerable to the attack.

181
00:19:11,430 --> 00:19:13,430
And so that wasn't even known that that was possible till recently.

182
00:19:13,440 --> 00:19:19,270
And I suspect there's lots of other fun surprises like that that we still don't know about remaining to happen.

183
00:19:19,270 --> 00:19:28,230
There's probably going to be ways to do that both through the mathematical properties like that one and bugs in the software itself as well where

184
00:19:28,230 --> 00:19:40,070
carefully curated and arranged training data with something incredibly subtle in it actually exploits a bug that causes the model to behave differently after it's trained in a way that's not entirely detectable yet today.

185
00:19:40,070 --> 00:19:48,950
which should get everyone a warm fuzzy feeling given the amount of places that these models are being deployed where safety is critical and security is critical.

