00:40:40,790 --> 00:40:47,030
And so part of that identity is that the model is the model that that you've allowed that you've authorized on your network.

391
00:40:47,030 --> 00:40:57,510
And then when that model does things like tool use or anything that it needs a token for to access other parts of your infrastructure, it's validated that that is the correct model.

392
00:40:57,510 --> 00:41:07,349
There's something that this doesn't take into account though and this is the intent of the user and the potential for indirect prompt injection as well.

393
00:41:07,349 --> 00:41:11,510
So the intent of the user actually matters in this case.

394
00:41:11,510 --> 00:41:20,309
So if you're going to give models access to internal infrastructure or other services, those services usually contain something valuable or do something.

395
00:41:20,309 --> 00:41:31,109
A really simple example is say Jira software development companies Jira very commonly used for bug tracking all kinds of information about how projects are meant to run and that kind of thing.

396
00:41:31,109 --> 00:41:36,550
You could see lots of use cases where a model needs to interact with Jira through a tool.

397
00:41:36,550 --> 00:41:46,230
Now does that workload get access to update tickets if someone has only asked it for information from a ticket rather than to actually update a ticket?

398
00:41:46,230 --> 00:41:48,630
And how do you know which identity it gets?

399
00:41:48,630 --> 00:41:55,190
Which token it gets like the token with access to write to Jira or just the token that gives it read access to Jira.

